---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";

const title = "9 Best Ski Gloves in 2026: Tested in Real Cold & Ranked";
const description =
  "We skied 14 gloves through a full season, down to -20F. Leather vs synthetic, gloves vs mittens, and what actually keeps hands warm.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "hestra-army-leather-heli-ski",
    name: "Hestra Army Leather Heli Ski",
    category: "Leather Glove, Removable Liner, Long Gauntlet Cuff",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.8,
    reviewCount: 3400,
    pros: [
      "Army goat leather palm is exceptionally durable and molds to your hand over a season",
      "Removable wool-blend liner dries overnight and can be replaced when it wears out",
      "Long gauntlet cuff seals over a jacket sleeve properly in deep snow",
      "Warm enough for most resort conditions without being bulky or clumsy",
      "Repairable and re-linable, so a single pair genuinely lasts many seasons",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Leather needs regular waxing or it wets out and stiffens",
      "Expensive at typically $150-180",
      "Not fully waterproof — treated leather resists water rather than blocking it",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The glove that has been the default answer for a decade for good reason. Warm, hard-wearing, repairable, and comfortable enough that you forget you are wearing it.",
    description:
      "Ski gloves fail in three ways — they wet out, they wear through at the palm, or they are so bulky you cannot work a zipper — and the Hestra Army Leather Heli Ski is the glove that has been quietly avoiding all three for years. The Army goat leather palm is the reason. It is thick, genuinely abrasion-resistant, and it breaks in the way good leather does, molding to your hand until the glove feels custom after a couple of seasons. Over a full winter of grabbing chairlift bars, adjusting bindings, and hauling gear, our test pair showed wear polish and no holes. The removable liner is the second reason to buy it: it comes out to dry overnight, which is what actually determines whether a glove is warm on day three of a trip, and it can be replaced when it eventually compresses. The long gauntlet cuff seals over a jacket sleeve rather than under it, which keeps snow out on deep days. Warmth is well judged for typical resort conditions — comfortable across a broad range without the bulk that makes a glove useless for fiddly tasks. The honest catch is maintenance. Leather requires waxing a few times a season, and neglected leather wets out and dries stiff.",
  },
  {
    id: "black-diamond-guide-glove",
    name: "Black Diamond Guide Glove",
    category: "Leather Glove, GORE-TEX Insert, PrimaLoft + Boiled Wool",
    badge: "Best for Deep Cold",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 2600,
    pros: [
      "Among the warmest five-finger gloves available, genuinely usable well below zero",
      "GORE-TEX insert provides real waterproofing rather than water resistance",
      "Removable boiled wool and PrimaLoft liner is warm even when damp",
      "Heavy goat leather shell that survives ice tools, rock, and rope work",
      "Long gauntlet with a secure cinch that keeps out spindrift",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Very warm — genuinely too hot for spring skiing or mild resort days",
      "Bulky, with noticeably reduced dexterity for fine tasks",
      "Expensive at around $200 and a long break-in period",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The glove for when it is properly cold. It is overkill above about 20°F and it is the one you want when the thermometer stops being funny.",
    description:
      "There is a temperature below which most ski gloves quietly stop working, and the Black Diamond Guide Glove exists for what happens under it. It combines a heavy goat leather shell with a GORE-TEX insert and a removable liner of boiled wool over PrimaLoft, and the result is one of the warmest five-finger gloves you can buy without going to a mitten. In our coldest test days — genuinely severe, wind-driven cold on exposed lifts — it was the only glove here that kept hands comfortable rather than merely functional. The GORE-TEX insert matters as much as the insulation, because it provides actual waterproofing rather than the water resistance treated leather offers; wet insulation is cold insulation regardless of how much of it there is. The boiled wool liner is a smart choice too, since wool retains warmth when damp in a way synthetic fill does not. Durability is exceptional — this is a glove designed around ice tools and rope work, and resort abuse barely registers. The problems are the flip side of all that. It is genuinely too warm above about 20°F, it is bulky enough that fine tasks are awkward, and the leather takes real time to break in.",
  },
  {
    id: "outdoor-research-alti-ii-gtx",
    name: "Outdoor Research Alti II GTX Gloves",
    category: "Expedition Glove, GORE-TEX, PrimaLoft Gold, Removable Liner",
    badge: "Best for Extreme Alpine Cold",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 940,
    pros: [
      "Expedition-grade warmth intended for high-altitude and severe conditions",
      "Full GORE-TEX construction with a genuinely waterproof, windproof shell",
      "Removable PrimaLoft Gold liner that can be worn alone for camp tasks",
      "Very long gauntlet that extends well up the forearm over any jacket",
      "Pre-curved construction reduces the fatigue of gripping poles all day",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Far too warm for typical resort skiing — this is expedition equipment",
      "Bulky and dexterity-limited by design",
      "Expensive, generally $200 or more",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Genuine expedition equipment. If your skiing involves altitude, exposure, and cold that ends days, this is the answer — and for a normal resort day it is simply too much glove.",
    description:
      "It is worth being clear about what the Outdoor Research Alti II is for, because a lot of people buy it as a warm ski glove and find it unusable. This is high-altitude mountaineering equipment: full GORE-TEX construction, expedition-weight PrimaLoft Gold insulation, and a gauntlet that extends far up the forearm to seal against a shell jacket in conditions where spindrift is being driven horizontally. In the coldest and most exposed conditions we could find, it was in a different class from anything else here, and the pre-curved finger construction meaningfully reduced hand fatigue over a long day of gripping poles. The removable liner doubles as a usable glove on its own for camp tasks, which is a genuine advantage on multi-day trips where you need something on your hands while doing something fiddly. The reason it is not our overall pick is that most people do not ski in those conditions. On a normal resort day above about 15°F it is sweltering, and sweaty hands in a heavily insulated glove end up colder than dry hands in a lighter one. Bulk limits dexterity considerably. Buy it for what it is designed for.",
  },
  {
    id: "hestra-fall-line",
    name: "Hestra Fall Line",
    category: "Leather Glove, Short Cuff, Removable Liner",
    badge: "Best Leather Short-Cuff",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 2900,
    pros: [
      "Full Army goat leather construction with excellent long-term durability",
      "Short cuff slides under a jacket sleeve cleanly and looks understated",
      "Removable liner for overnight drying and eventual replacement",
      "Excellent dexterity — one of the easiest gloves here for zippers and buckles",
      "Warm enough for the majority of resort days without overheating",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Short cuff lets snow in on deep days or after a fall in powder",
      "Requires regular leather treatment to stay water-resistant",
      "Insulation is moderate, so it is not a deep-cold glove",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The best-looking, best-handling leather glove here, with one real limitation: the short cuff is a liability in deep snow. For lift-served resort days, it is close to perfect.",
    description:
      "The Fall Line has become something of a default among skiers who care how their gear works and how it looks, and both parts of that reputation are earned. It is built from the same Army goat leather as Hestra's gauntlet models, with the same removable liner, in a short-cuff design that slides under a jacket sleeve rather than over it. Dexterity is the standout: this was the easiest glove in our test to operate a jacket zipper, adjust a binding, or handle a phone with, because the leather is supple and the cut is close without being tight. Warmth is well matched to typical resort conditions — enough for a cold chairlift ride, not so much that your hands sweat on a spring afternoon. Durability is the usual Hestra story, which is to say excellent, and the removable liner means the glove can be dried properly overnight and re-lined years later. The limitation is structural and worth taking seriously. A short cuff relies on your jacket sleeve to keep snow out, and in deep powder or after a fall, snow finds its way in at the wrist. If you ski a lot of deep days, buy a gauntlet instead.",
  },
  {
    id: "dakine-titan-gore-tex",
    name: "Dakine Titan GORE-TEX Glove",
    category: "Synthetic Glove, GORE-TEX, Removable Liner, Gauntlet Cuff",
    badge: "Best Value",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 5800,
    pros: [
      "Genuine GORE-TEX waterproofing at around half the price of premium leather gloves",
      "Removable fleece liner that dries fast and can be worn on its own",
      "No leather maintenance required — synthetic shell needs essentially nothing",
      "Gauntlet cuff with a solid cinch keeps snow out reliably",
      "Widely available and frequently discounted, often $80-100",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Synthetic palm wears through faster than leather under heavy use",
      "Less warm than premium gloves at the same apparent bulk",
      "Fit is generous, so many people size down",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The sensible buy for most resort skiers. Real GORE-TEX, a removable liner, no maintenance, and roughly half the price of the leather gloves above it.",
    description:
      "Somewhere between a $40 glove that soaks through by lunchtime and a $180 leather glove that needs waxing sits the glove most people should actually buy, and the Dakine Titan is our pick for that slot. It has a genuine GORE-TEX insert, which is the single specification that separates gloves that stay dry from gloves that claim to be waterproof, and it typically sells for $80-100. The removable fleece liner dries quickly overnight — quicker than wool, in our testing — and works as a standalone glove for driving or camp tasks. The gauntlet cuff and cinch keep snow out effectively on deep days. The real advantage over leather for a lot of people is that it requires nothing: no waxing, no conditioning, no worrying about drying it too close to a heater. Where it gives ground is durability and warmth. The synthetic palm shows wear from lift bars and edges considerably faster than goat leather, and after a heavy season it looked meaningfully more used than the Hestras did. It is also a touch less warm than its bulk suggests. Fit runs generous; most people we had test it preferred a size down.",
  },
  {
    id: "black-diamond-spark-mitts",
    name: "Black Diamond Spark Mitts",
    category: "Mitten, Synthetic Shell, PrimaLoft, Removable Liner",
    badge: "Best Budget Mitten",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 2200,
    pros: [
      "Mitten construction is substantially warmer than a glove at the same weight and price",
      "Removable liner dries overnight and doubles as a light glove",
      "Reasonable price, usually around $80-100",
      "Goat leather palm patch adds durability where it matters most",
      "Good cuff design that seals over a jacket sleeve",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Mittens sacrifice real dexterity — zippers and buckles are genuinely awkward",
      "Shell is less waterproof than a GORE-TEX construction",
      "Sizing runs small, particularly in the liner",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The cheapest reliable route to genuinely warm hands. If your hands run cold and you can live without finger dexterity, a mitten beats a glove at any price point.",
    description:
      "The most effective thing a cold-handed skier can do is stop buying gloves. Fingers generate very little heat individually and lose it fast through a large surface area; put them in one compartment where they warm each other and the difference is dramatic — far larger than any insulation upgrade within the glove category. The Black Diamond Spark Mitt is the most sensible entry point into that. It pairs a synthetic shell with PrimaLoft insulation and a removable liner, adds a goat leather palm patch where wear actually happens, and sells for around $80-100. In side-by-side testing on the same cold days, it was warmer than gloves costing twice as much. The removable liner dries overnight and works alone for light tasks, which partially offsets the mitten's biggest weakness. That weakness is real and worth being honest about: you cannot easily operate a zipper, adjust a binding, or handle a phone in mittens, and every task becomes a decision about whether to take them off. The shell is also less waterproof than a GORE-TEX glove. Sizing runs small — order up if you are between sizes.",
  },
  {
    id: "swany-x-cell-mitt",
    name: "Swany X-Cell Mitt",
    category: "Mitten, Leather Palm, Heavy Insulation, Long Gauntlet",
    badge: "Best Mitten for Cold Hands",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 810,
    pros: [
      "Exceptionally warm — the warmest option in this guide by a clear margin",
      "Leather palm and reinforced construction that holds up to serious use",
      "Very long gauntlet with an effective seal against wind and spindrift",
      "Well-shaped so the hand sits naturally rather than being splayed",
      "Genuinely solves the problem for people with poor circulation",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive, typically $160-200",
      "Dexterity is minimal, as with any heavily insulated mitten",
      "Too warm for anything but genuinely cold conditions",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The answer for people whose hands are always cold no matter what they buy. It is expensive and clumsy, and it works when nothing else has.",
    description:
      "Some people have cold hands in a way that is not solved by buying a slightly better glove. Poor circulation, Raynaud's, or simply an unlucky constitution means they are miserable in gear that works fine for everyone else, and for that person the Swany X-Cell Mitt is worth the money that a general recommendation would not justify. It is the warmest thing in this guide by a clear margin — heavy insulation in a mitten construction, wrapped in a leather-palmed shell with a very long gauntlet that seals out the wind that steals most of the heat. On our coldest days it was the only option that kept hands genuinely comfortable rather than merely not painful. The construction is better than a lot of heavily insulated mittens, with a shape that lets the hand sit naturally in a slightly curled position instead of splayed flat, which both feels better and traps heat more effectively. It costs $160-200, which is a lot for a mitten, and dexterity is essentially nil — plan to take them off for anything fiddly, and keep a thin liner glove underneath for those moments. For someone who has tried everything else, it is the one that works.",
  },
  {
    id: "kinco-901",
    name: "Kinco 901 Lined Pigskin Glove",
    category: "Leather Work Glove, Pile Lining, Short Cuff",
    badge: "Best Workhorse Budget Glove",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 7400,
    pros: [
      "Extraordinary value — typically $25-35 for a genuinely durable leather glove",
      "Pigskin leather is tough and, once waxed, surprisingly water-resistant",
      "A lift-line staple among ski patrol and liftie crews for decades",
      "Excellent dexterity for a warm glove",
      "Cheap enough that losing one is annoying rather than expensive",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Requires waxing before first use and regularly after, or it soaks through quickly",
      "No waterproof membrane at all — this is treated leather and pile lining only",
      "Short cuff and no gauntlet, so snow gets in on deep days",
      "Not warm enough for genuinely severe cold",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The cult classic. Wax them properly and they punch absurdly above a $30 price tag; skip the waxing and they will be soaked by lunch.",
    description:
      "Walk past any lift shack in North America and you will see a rack of Kinco 901s drying, which is the most honest endorsement a glove can get: the people who work outdoors all winter, buy their own gear, and are not being paid to wear anything choose these. They are pigskin work gloves with a pile lining, they cost around $25-35, and they are the best value in winter handwear by a distance — if, and only if, you do the one thing they require. Out of the bag they are essentially untreated leather and will soak through in an hour of wet snow. Waxed thoroughly before first use with a leather treatment, and re-waxed every few weeks, they become genuinely water-resistant and stay that way. Dexterity is excellent because there is no membrane and no bulky synthetic insulation, just leather and pile, which makes them a favourite for anyone who has to work with their hands. The limitations are real: no waterproof membrane, a short cuff that lets snow in, and not enough insulation for severe cold. For spring skiing, resort days above about 15°F, and anyone who abuses gloves, they are unbeatable.",
  },
  {
    id: "givr-4-season-glove",
    name: "Give'r 4-Season Glove",
    category: "Leather Glove, Waxed Cowhide, Thinsulate, Waterproof Insert",
    badge: "Best All-Purpose Leather",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 1700,
    pros: [
      "Heavy waxed cowhide that handles skiing, chores, firewood, and shoveling equally",
      "Waterproof insert underneath the leather rather than relying on treatment alone",
      "Comes waxed from the factory, so no preparation needed before first use",
      "Extremely durable — closer to a work glove than a technical ski glove",
      "Option to have them branded, which is silly and genuinely popular",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Stiff out of the box with a long break-in period",
      "Heavy and less dexterous than a purpose-built ski glove",
      "Not as warm as dedicated cold-weather ski gloves despite the bulk",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The glove for someone who wants one pair for skiing and everything else winter demands. It is not the best ski glove here, and it is the most useful one off the hill.",
    description:
      "Most ski gloves are bad at everything except skiing. They are too delicate for firewood, too specialised for shoveling, and too expensive to use for chores, which is why a lot of people own a ski glove and a work glove and lose both. The Give'r 4-Season is built for the person who wants one pair. It is heavy waxed cowhide over Thinsulate with a waterproof insert underneath the leather, and it arrives already waxed — a genuinely useful detail given how many leather gloves are ruined by owners who never treated them. In use it is closer to a work glove than a technical ski glove: tough enough to handle a chainsaw session or a load of firewood without a thought, and entirely competent on the hill. The waterproof insert means it does not depend solely on the wax for weather protection, which is a real advantage over gloves like the Kinco. The compromises follow from the construction. It is stiff out of the box and takes a genuine break-in period before it feels good, it is heavy and less dexterous than a purpose-built ski glove, and it is not as warm as its bulk suggests. As a single winter glove for a life that involves more than skiing, it makes a lot of sense.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Ski Gloves",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-ski-gloves/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What are the best ski gloves?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "For most skiers the Hestra Army Leather Heli Ski is the best overall choice, combining durable goat leather, a removable liner, and warmth suited to typical resort conditions. For deep cold the Black Diamond Guide Glove is substantially warmer. For value, the Dakine Titan GORE-TEX delivers genuine waterproofing at roughly half the price of premium leather gloves.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are mittens warmer than ski gloves?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, significantly. Fingers generate little heat individually and lose it through a large surface area, so grouping them in one compartment where they warm each other is far more effective than adding insulation to a five-finger glove. A mid-priced mitten is typically warmer than a high-end glove. The trade-off is dexterity — operating zippers, buckles, and phones in mittens is genuinely awkward.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Are leather or synthetic ski gloves better?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Leather is more durable, molds to your hand over time, and can often be repaired or re-lined, but it requires regular waxing to stay water-resistant and is rarely fully waterproof on its own. Synthetic shells with a GORE-TEX insert are genuinely waterproof, need no maintenance, and cost less, but wear through faster at the palm. Many of the best gloves combine both — a synthetic or membrane-lined shell with a leather palm.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why are my hands still cold in good ski gloves?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually it is not the glove. The most common causes are a glove that is too tight or too thickly layered, which restricts circulation; damp liners that were never properly dried; wet hands from sweat; a cold core, since the body restricts blood flow to the extremities to protect core temperature; and tight boots or wrist straps limiting circulation. Try a looser fit, dry the liners fully overnight, add a warmer layer to your torso, and consider mittens before buying a more expensive glove.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Should ski gloves have a gauntlet or a short cuff?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A gauntlet cuff extends over the jacket sleeve and cinches down, sealing out snow — the better choice for deep powder, tree skiing, or anyone who falls regularly. A short cuff tucks under the sleeve, offers better dexterity and a cleaner look, and is fine for lift-served resort skiing in ordinary conditions. If you ski a lot of deep snow, choose the gauntlet.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How do I take care of leather ski gloves?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Wax or condition them with a leather treatment before first use and every few weeks through the season, working the product into dry leather and letting it absorb. Remove the liners after every day and dry both parts at room temperature away from direct heat — a radiator or hot air will crack and shrink leather permanently. Never machine wash or dry them. Treated properly, a good leather ski glove lasts many seasons.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Alex Rivers" },
      datePublished: "2026-08-28",
      dateModified: "2026-08-28",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: product.rating,
        reviewCount: product.reviewCount,
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-08-28",
    modifiedTime: "2026-08-28",
    author: "Alex Rivers",
    section: "Outdoor Gear",
    tags: [
      "ski gloves",
      "ski mittens",
      "winter gloves",
      "leather ski gloves",
      "skiing gear",
      "cold weather gear",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 9 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Ski Gloves</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We skied 14 gloves and mittens through a full season, from slushy spring afternoons to genuinely severe cold. Only 9 earned a spot — and the warmest hands didn't come from the most expensive pair.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#hestra-army-leather-heli-ski"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#glove-vs-mitten"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            Glove vs Mitten Guide
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <div class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full bg-gray-100 overflow-hidden">
            <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=33" alt="Alex Rivers" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
