        <div id="glove-vs-mitten" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Gloves vs Mittens vs 3-Finger
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the first decision and the one that affects warmth more than any other, including insulation weight, shell material, and price.
          </p>

          <p>
            The physics is simple. A finger on its own is a small volume of tissue with a large surface area, generating very little heat and losing it quickly. Four fingers sharing one compartment warm each other and present a much smaller surface area to the cold. That effect is large — in our side-by-side testing on the same days, a mid-priced mitten was consistently warmer than gloves costing twice as much. If your hands run cold, switching from gloves to mittens will do more than any amount of extra spending within the glove category.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Gloves</strong> win on dexterity, and dexterity matters more than people credit. Adjusting a binding, zipping a jacket, handling a phone, buckling a boot — every one of those is trivial in gloves and genuinely awkward in mittens, and the temptation to take mittens off for each task is how hands actually get cold. For most skiers in most conditions, a good glove is the right default.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Mittens</strong> win on outright warmth by a clear margin. They are the answer for genuinely cold days, for people with poor circulation, and for anyone who has tried warmer and warmer gloves without success.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Three-finger designs</strong>, sometimes called lobster gloves, split the difference by pairing fingers together while keeping the index finger and thumb independent. They are warmer than gloves and more usable than mittens, and they look unusual enough that many people rule them out on appearance alone. If warmth and function both matter and you do not care how they look, they are a genuinely smart compromise.
          </p>

