        <div id="insulation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Insulation, Membranes and What "Waterproof" Means
          </h2>

          <p>
            Three separate things determine whether a glove keeps your hands warm and dry, and marketing copy tends to blur them together.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Insulation</h3>
          <p>
            Most ski gloves use synthetic fill — PrimaLoft and Thinsulate being the common names — which insulates well, is compressible, and importantly retains some warmth when damp. Wool and boiled wool liners are less common and perform notably better when wet, which is why they appear in high-end cold-weather gloves. Down is rare in gloves for the obvious reason that hands get wet and wet down is worthless.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Membranes</h3>
          <p>
            This is the specification that separates gloves that stay dry from gloves that claim to. A waterproof-breathable membrane — GORE-TEX being the benchmark, with various proprietary alternatives — is a thin layer between the shell and the insulation that blocks liquid water while letting vapour out. A glove without one is relying on the shell material and its treatment alone, which works until it does not.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water Resistance vs Waterproof</h3>
          <p>
            Treated leather is water-resistant: it repels water for a while, then wets out and needs re-treating. A membrane-equipped glove is waterproof: water does not get through at all as long as the membrane is intact. Neither is automatically better. Leather breathes more, lasts longer, and molds to your hand; membranes need no maintenance and do not fail if you forget to wax them. The best gloves often combine a membrane with a leather palm, getting durability where wear happens and waterproofing everywhere.
          </p>

          <p>
            One consequence worth knowing: <strong>a glove can be perfectly waterproof and still end up soaked from the inside.</strong> Hands sweat, and a heavily insulated glove with a membrane can trap that moisture against the skin. That is why breathability matters, why removable liners are so valuable, and why buying the warmest glove available frequently makes hands colder rather than warmer.
          </p>
        </div>

