        <div id="leather-vs-synthetic" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Leather vs Synthetic Shells
          </h2>

          <p>
            This choice determines how long the glove lasts, how much attention it needs, and how it feels after a season.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Leather</h3>
          <p>
            Goat leather is the premium choice and pigskin the budget one, and both share the qualities that make leather worth it: outstanding abrasion resistance, excellent grip, and a tendency to mold to the individual hand until the glove feels custom. Leather also breathes better than a membrane, so hands stay drier from the inside on warmer days. The cost is maintenance. Untreated leather wets out quickly and dries stiff and shrunken, and the only prevention is waxing regularly. A leather glove that gets waxed lasts many seasons; one that does not is finished in a single winter.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Synthetic</h3>
          <p>
            Nylon and polyester shells with a waterproof-breathable membrane need nothing from you. They are waterproof out of the box and stay that way, they cost less, and they dry faster. What they give up is durability at the palm, where lift bars, edges, and pole grips concentrate wear — a synthetic palm shows real damage in the time a leather one shows polish. They also tend to feel less pleasant, though that is subjective.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Hybrid Answer</h3>
          <p>
            Most of the best gloves use both: a synthetic shell with a membrane over the back of the hand, and a leather palm and fingers where abrasion happens. This gets the waterproofing of a membrane and the durability of leather, at the cost of still needing to treat the leather sections occasionally. If you are unsure which camp you are in, a hybrid is the safe answer.
          </p>

