        <div id="fit-and-cuffs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Fit, Cuffs and Dexterity
          </h2>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Fit</h3>
          <p>
            A ski glove should have a small amount of air space at the fingertips — roughly a few millimetres — because that trapped air is doing insulating work and because a glove pressed tight against the skin conducts heat away and restricts circulation. This is the most common sizing error: people buy gloves that feel snug and reassuring in a shop and then have cold hands all season. If your fingertips press against the end, size up.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Gauntlet vs Short Cuff</h3>
          <p>
            A gauntlet cuff extends over the jacket sleeve and cinches down, which is the reliable way to keep snow out. It is the right choice for deep powder, tree skiing, and anyone who falls with any regularity. A short cuff tucks under the sleeve, gives better wrist mobility and a cleaner look, and is entirely adequate for ordinary lift-served days on groomed snow. The failure mode of a short cuff is specific and memorable: a fall in deep snow packs it straight into the wrist opening.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Dexterity</h3>
          <p>
            Dexterity is a warmth feature, not a convenience one. Every task that forces you to remove a glove exposes a warm hand to cold air and often to snow, and each removal costs more heat than the glove will regain in ten minutes. A glove you can zip a jacket in keeps your hands warmer over a day than a warmer glove you keep taking off. Pre-curved construction, a supple palm, and a well-shaped thumb all contribute here.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Wrist Leashes and Removable Liners</h3>
          <p>
            Leashes are worth using — a glove dropped from a chairlift is gone, and that is a genuinely common way to lose one. Removable liners are worth paying for, because they are what makes proper overnight drying possible and because they can be replaced when they compress, extending the life of a glove by years.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6 -->
        <div id="cold-hands" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Why Your Hands Are Still Cold
          </h2>

          <p>
            People with cold hands tend to buy progressively more expensive gloves and stay cold, because in most cases the glove was never the problem. Work through these before spending more.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Warm Your Core, Not Your Hands</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              When your core temperature drops, your body restricts blood flow to the extremities to protect your vital organs. Cold hands are frequently a symptom of an underdressed torso rather than an underdressed hand. Adding a warm mid-layer or a proper hat will do more for cold fingers than upgrading from a $90 glove to a $180 one — and it costs less.
            </p>
          </div>

          <p>
            <strong>Your gloves are too tight.</strong> Compression kills circulation, and circulation is what delivers heat to your fingers. This includes layering a thick liner inside a glove that was already a snug fit — a very common self-inflicted problem. Air space insulates; pressure does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Your liners never actually dried.</strong> A liner that feels dry on the surface can hold significant moisture, and damp insulation conducts heat away from your hand all day. Pull removable liners out every single evening. If your gloves do not have removable liners, they need longer to dry than a single night, which is why a second pair on multi-day trips matters more than a warmer single pair.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Your hands are sweating.</strong> An over-insulated glove on a moderate day traps sweat, and evaporating sweat cools skin efficiently. Being too warm early in the day is a reliable route to being cold later. Match the glove to the conditions rather than buying for the worst day of the season.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Something is restricting circulation upstream.</strong> Tight boot buckles, a tight wrist leash, a watch strap, or a jacket cuff that binds all reduce blood flow before it reaches the hand. So does gripping poles tightly all day — consciously relaxing your grip on a lift ride helps more than it sounds like it should.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Then, if all of that is handled, switch to mittens.</strong> Not a warmer glove — mittens. It is a bigger step change than anything else available, and it costs less than the next glove up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 7 -->
        <div id="care" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Drying, Waxing and Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ski gloves are usually killed by their owners rather than worn out, and almost always in one of two ways: dried on a heater, or never waxed.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Dry them at room temperature, away from direct heat.</strong> A radiator, a wood stove, a car heater vent, or a boot dryer on a high setting will cook leather — it shrinks, stiffens, and cracks, and the damage is permanent. Pull the liners out, open everything up, and let them dry slowly overnight in ordinary room air. If you must accelerate it, use a fan rather than heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wax leather before first use and regularly through the season.</strong> Most leather ski gloves ship untreated or lightly treated, and the single most common complaint about them — that they soaked through on day one — is almost always an untreated pair. Work a leather wax or conditioner into dry leather with your fingers, let it absorb, and repeat every few weeks. It takes ten minutes and it is the difference between a glove that lasts five seasons and one that lasts one.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wash sparingly and never in a machine.</strong> Wipe the shell with clean water and mild soap if needed. Liners can usually be hand-washed gently and air dried; check the manufacturer's guidance first. Machine washing and tumble drying will destroy both the membrane and the leather.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Store them dry, and store them somewhere dry.</strong> Gloves packed away damp at the end of a season come out mildewed, and mildew in a liner is effectively unfixable. This is also where storage location matters: a garage on an unsealed concrete slab wicks moisture upward continuously, and gear stored in a bin on that floor absorbs it all summer. Sealing the slab fixes the underlying problem — our <a href="/best-garage-floor-epoxy/">garage floor epoxy guide</a> covers the coatings that work — and a <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">dehumidifier sized for the space</a> handles humid storage areas. Store gloves fully dry, in a breathable container, off the floor.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 8: FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What are the best ski gloves?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most skiers the Hestra Army Leather Heli Ski is the best overall choice, combining durable goat leather, a removable liner, and warmth suited to typical resort conditions. For deep cold the Black Diamond Guide Glove is substantially warmer. For value, the Dakine Titan GORE-TEX delivers genuine waterproofing at roughly half the price of premium leather gloves.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are mittens warmer than ski gloves?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, significantly. Fingers generate little heat individually and lose it through a large surface area, so grouping them in one compartment where they warm each other is far more effective than adding insulation to a five-finger glove. A mid-priced mitten is typically warmer than a high-end glove. The trade-off is dexterity — operating zippers, buckles, and phones in mittens is genuinely awkward.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are leather or synthetic ski gloves better?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Leather is more durable, molds to your hand over time, and can often be repaired or re-lined, but it requires regular waxing to stay water-resistant and is rarely fully waterproof on its own. Synthetic shells with a GORE-TEX insert are genuinely waterproof, need no maintenance, and cost less, but wear through faster at the palm. Many of the best gloves combine both — a synthetic or membrane-lined shell with a leather palm.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why are my hands still cold in good gloves?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually it is not the glove. The most common causes are a glove that is too tight or too thickly layered, which restricts circulation; damp liners that were never properly dried; wet hands from sweat; a cold core, since the body restricts blood flow to the extremities to protect core temperature; and tight boots or wrist straps limiting circulation. Try a looser fit, dry the liners fully overnight, add a warmer layer to your torso, and consider mittens before buying a more expensive glove.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Gauntlet or short cuff?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A gauntlet cuff extends over the jacket sleeve and cinches down, sealing out snow — the better choice for deep powder, tree skiing, or anyone who falls regularly. A short cuff tucks under the sleeve, offers better dexterity and a cleaner look, and is fine for lift-served resort skiing in ordinary conditions. If you ski a lot of deep snow, choose the gauntlet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I take care of leather ski gloves?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wax or condition them with a leather treatment before first use and every few weeks through the season, working the product into dry leather and letting it absorb. Remove the liners after every day and dry both parts at room temperature away from direct heat — a radiator or hot air will crack and shrink leather permanently. Never machine wash or dry them. Treated properly, a good leather ski glove lasts many seasons.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes When Buying Ski Gloves
          </h2>

          <p>
            These four account for most of the cold hands and most of the prematurely dead gloves we see, and none of them require spending more money to fix.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Gloves That Fit Snugly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A snug glove feels quality in a shop and is cold on the hill. Trapped air is insulation, and compression restricts the blood flow that actually delivers heat to your fingers. You want a few millimetres of space at the fingertips. This is the single most common cause of cold hands in expensive gloves.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Drying Gloves on a Heater</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Direct heat shrinks and cracks leather permanently and can delaminate a membrane. Every season people destroy a $180 pair of gloves on a radiator overnight. Pull the liners, open everything up, and dry slowly at room temperature — use a fan if you need it faster, never heat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Never Waxing Leather Gloves</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most leather ski gloves ship untreated or barely treated, and the complaint that they leaked on the first day is nearly always an untreated pair. Ten minutes with a leather wax before the season and every few weeks after is the difference between five seasons and one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for the Coldest Possible Day</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                An expedition-weight glove on a 25°F resort day makes your hands sweat, and damp hands get cold fast. Buy for the conditions you actually ski most of the time, and if you genuinely need coverage for extremes, own two pairs rather than one compromise pair — a mid-weight glove and a warm mitten covers everything better than a single expensive glove.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

